## 3. F-1 (BLOCKING) — inventory B scans a narrower shape than the brief specifies

  ROOT CAUSE, one line:
    packages/cli/src/containment.test.ts:205
        if (exprText.endsWith("Id")) {

  THE SPEC it diverges from, studio/briefs/W-047.md:363-365:
    "Inventory B — raw id-to-path joins. Every `TemplateExpression` with at
     least one substitution whose final literal chunk ends in `.md`, anywhere
     in the source set, yielding `${rel}:${fn}#${nth}` plus a disposition"

  There is no `*Id`-suffix condition in the spec. The implementation adds one and
  filters on the *source text* of the last substitution
  (containment.test.ts:202-205), so the scan sees a raw join only when the id is
  spelled with a trailing "Id".

  ONE ROOT CAUSE, BUT A CLASS OF MISSES. What I searched and what I found:
  I re-implemented inventory B's scan exactly as the brief specifies it (no "Id"
  filter), ran it over the same source set — every non-test, non-.d.ts `.ts` under
  packages/, apps/ and adapters/, excluding node_modules and dist — and diffed
  against the committed implementation. 17 template joins at HEAD match the
  brief's rule and are silently dropped:

    apps/server/src/store.ts:224            expr "id"        `${id}.md`
    packages/cli/src/init.ts:108            expr "isoDateInZone(now, timezone)"
    packages/cli/src/new.ts:68              expr "id"        `${id}.md`
    packages/cli/src/new.ts:179             expr "id"        `briefs/${id}.md`
    packages/cli/src/new.ts:194             expr "id"        `${id}.md`
    packages/cli/src/new.ts:196             expr "id"        `${id}.md`
    packages/cli/src/retro.ts:395           expr "id"        `lessons/${id}.md`
    packages/cli/src/retro.ts:431           expr "pid"       `petitiones/${pid}.md`
    packages/cli/src/retro.ts:534           expr "cascade"   `acta/${dateStr}-retro-${cascade}.md`
    packages/cli/src/tick.ts:246            expr "sella"     `${today}-${sella}-daily.md`
    packages/commands/src/context.ts:187    expr "d.id"      `acta/${d.id}.md`
    packages/commands/src/talk.ts:187       expr "id"        `${id}.md`
    packages/commands/src/talk.ts:206       expr "slug"      `${date}-${slug}.md`
    packages/commands/src/talk.ts:207       expr "n"         `${date}-${slug}-${n}.md`
    packages/commands/src/writes.ts:107     expr "id"        `${id}.md`
    packages/commands/src/writes.ts:679     expr "slug"      `${dateStr}-${slug}.md`
    packages/commands/src/writes.ts:680     expr "n"         `${dateStr}-${slug}-${n}.md`

  Most are almost certainly `derived` — nextId output, dates, slugs. That is not
  the point. The brief's design is scan wide, then pin a DISPOSITION per entry
  (`guarded` / `derived` / `bypass`); the implementation instead narrows the SCAN
  by an undocumented heuristic so the pinned list stays at 12. Those are different
  guards. One of the 17 (writes.ts:107) is safeItemPath's own join; several others
  (store.ts:224, talk.ts:187) I did not adjudicate, because adjudicating them is
  exactly the work the brief assigned and it has not been done.

  FOUR CONSEQUENCES, each verified:

  1. The acceptance criterion assigned to me (brief:540) fails as written:
     "adding a raw ``join(x, "opera", `${id}.md`)`` anywhere must fail inventory B."
     Measured in section 2(e): exit 0, 0 FAIL. It does not fail.

  2. Red 04 does not demonstrate what it claims. mF's "dead" verdict is an
     artifact of the harness naming its own probe parameter `probeOpusId`
     (scripts/safe-item-path-mutants.mjs:251):
        "export function __w047MutantProbeF(dir: string, probeOpusId: string):
         string {\n  return `${dir}/${probeOpusId}.md`;\n}"
     Rename that parameter to `id` and mF survives. The mutant tests the "Id"
     filter, not the property behaviour 4 exists to prove.

  3. The opus's own stated purpose for inventory B is not achieved. brief:63:
     "see behaviour 4, whose second inventory pins every entry above so a ninth
     bypass cannot appear unnoticed." brief:560: "Inventory B pins them meanwhile,
     with dispositions, so the debt is visible and countable rather than implied."
     A ninth bypass written as ``join(dir, `${id}.md`)`` appears unnoticed today.

  4. The risk is live, not hypothetical, and lands on the follow-up this opus
     files. The repo's own naming is split: branch.ts / close.ts / verify.ts use
     `opusId`; new.ts / talk.ts / retro.ts / store.ts use `id` / `pid` / `slug`.
     W-059 routes verify, close, branch, merge and prune through the helper. A
     builder who renames a local while refactoring one of those five silently
     drops its inventory-B pin, and the census stays green.

  AGGRAVATING — the amendment channel was live and was not used for this.
  The brief instructs (brief:384-387): "The builder verifies each disposition
  against the source before pinning it and reports any disagreement with this
  table rather than editing the source." The builder did exactly that for the
  prune.ts function name — pinned the scan's answer and filed an inline NOTE at
  containment.test.ts:245-250 — and the architect amended the brief for it in
  683a5c7. The inventory-B narrowing got no such report. It appears only as a
  descriptive header comment (containment.test.ts:12-14) phrased as if it were
  the spec: "a template literal whose last substitution is an identifier ending
  in 'Id' and whose last literal chunk ends in '.md' — the exact shape of the
  repo-wide scan the brief's Intent describes." It is not the shape the brief
  describes. The brief's own red-team prompt (brief:610-613) asked precisely this
  question — "Name a bypass shape it cannot see" — and the answer went unrecorded.

  WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT. Either is legitimate; both belong to the architect, not
  the builder acting alone:
    (i) drop the `endsWith("Id")` condition, re-pin inventory B with the ~29
        entries the brief's rule yields and a disposition for each, re-record
        red 04, rename mF's probe parameter to `id` so the mutant tests the
        property rather than the filter; or
    (ii) amend the brief to specify the narrowed derivation, state the bypass
        shapes it cannot see, and correct acceptance check (e) — which weakens
        the opus's central claim and should be signed, not assumed.

