## 5. RULING — the red re-record: HONEST CORRECTION, not evidence backfilling

  WHAT I OBSERVED. All four logs carry the same tree hash
  `# tree: dirty:f51320c5e597de3a948c0a06bb1f09a58b9ba148` and timestamps
  07:26:14, 07:26:16, 07:26:22, 07:26:24 — four reds recorded within ten seconds
  of each other from one tree state that already held all four behaviours'
  tests. The trace is visible in the evidence itself: 01.log's mB mutant prints
  `FAIL row1 handoff:` through `FAIL row5 greenlight:` alongside the contract
  rows, because behaviour 2's block existed when 01 was recorded.

  I RULE IT HONEST CORRECTION. Five reasons, in order of weight:

  1. The defect was in the recorder, not in the recorded behaviour. The mutant
     set, the mutations, the assertions and their outcomes are unchanged; only
     the transcript's leading whitespace moved. The house rule "never run red
     twice for one behaviour" exists to stop a builder re-running until the
     RESULT looks right. Here the result was identical before and after.

  2. The correction is verifiable at HEAD, not merely asserted. The committed
     harness carries its own reason, in source, where the next builder will read
     it (scripts/safe-item-path-mutants.mjs, the print loop):
        "Printed verbatim, never indented: these are the red's assertion-level
         content, and the acceptance gate greps the log for `^FAIL` lines — an
         indented copy would never match."
     That is the fix and its rationale committed as code, which is the studio's
     preferred form over prose. I re-ran the harness (section 1) and got content
     consistent with the committed logs.

  3. A log whose FAIL lines cannot be grepped is not evidence. The brief's own
     acceptance (brief:526-532) greps `^FAIL`; the pairwise `sed '1,/^$/d'` diffs
     read the same bodies. Indented logs would have passed every eye and failed
     every gate. Re-recording was the only way to produce evidence that could be
     checked at all — it made the reds MORE checkable, not less.

  4. The logs were not scrubbed to look like first-take reds. 01.log openly
     contains behaviour 2's labels. Backfilling hides its own seam; this did the
     opposite, and the seam is what let me confirm the account independently.

  5. It was caught before any review and self-reported rather than found by me.

  WHAT WOULD HAVE MADE IT CLEANER, for the retro: record the deviation in the
  traditio (F-4), and re-record each red from the tree its own behaviour was
  written at rather than all four from the final tree. The latter is cosmetic
  for a mutation-record red — the claim is "mutant X kills labels Y", which does
  not depend on which other tests exist — but it would have kept each log's body
  scoped to its own behaviour.

