## 7. The contract table (behaviour 1) — read against what the brief promises

  packages/cli/src/writes.test.ts, behaviour 1 block. Helper under test:
  packages/commands/src/writes.ts:104-110.

  Shape guard is `!id || /[\\/]/.test(id) || id === "." || id === ".."`.
  Every disjunct has at least one row:
    empty          `""`
    "." segment    `.`
    ".." segment   `..`, `../W-001`, `../../etc/passwd`, `opera/../../x`
    "/" separator  `a/b`, `/etc/passwd`, `../W-001`, `opera/../../x`
    "\" separator  `..\..\win.ini`, `C:\Windows\win.ini`
  Absolute paths: `/etc/passwd` (POSIX) and `C:\Windows\win.ini` (Windows shape).
  The brief's promised coverage — dot-dot, absolute, separators, empty, the shape
  guard — is met, with no disjunct resting on a single row.

  NON-SANITIZATION is proved the right way round, on both sides:
    accepted rows assert `basename(r) === id + ".md"` — the id is used verbatim;
    refused rows assert `typeof r !== "string"` — a refusal is never a quiet
    rewrite. `../W-001` is a REFUSED row, so mC (sanitize-in-helper) cannot pass
    by laundering it into `W-001.md`; 01.log confirms mC kills 10 rows including
    every refused one.
  The accepted-and-contained rows (`..W-001`, `%2e%2e%2fW-001`, U+2215 `∕W-001`,
  `W-001\u0000x`) pin the harder half — that the helper decodes nothing and
  normalizes nothing, and that containment holds anyway. The NUL row additionally
  asserts `existsSync(r)` does not throw. `..W-001` correctly documents that ".."
  is a prefix here, not a segment.
  The two base-shape assertions (trailing separator, cwd-relative base) ride the
  block as specified, both by JSON-identity against the canonical answer.

  ONE weak row: F-2, the vacuous `r.error.includes("")`.
  Otherwise the table covers what the brief promises and mD's `expect: alive`
  reading is correct — with the shape guard in place, `dirname(join(resolvedDir,
  id + ".md"))` cannot differ from `resolvedDir`, so removing only that check is
  unobservable. Carrying it as a RUN mutant rather than a prose claim is the
  right device, and 01.log records `exit=0 FAIL-count=0 -> matched (alive)`.

  THE IN-DIRECTORY COLLISION SENTINELS prove non-sanitization as designed.
  seedSentinels (writes.test.ts) and seedOpusSentinels (lifecycle.test.ts:110-117)
  each build both targets, so the three landing places are distinguished:
    raw join       -> `<dir>/W-777.md`         (outside sentinel)
    sanitizing     -> `<dir>/opera/W-777.md`   (collision record)
    safeItemPath   -> neither; refuses
  Each of the nine rows asserts exit 2, the guard's own `unknown opus/petitio:
  ../W-777` on stderr, BOTH sentinels byte-identical, and no event appended —
  five assertions, which is what makes the stderr discriminator do real work.
  02.log and 03.log confirm both halves bite: under the raw-join mutant
  `row1 handoff: outside sentinel byte-identical` FAILs; under row6's mutant the
  stderr line FAILs with the mutant's own wording
  (`../W-777 is not greenlit or halted (state: building)`), which is the
  discriminator W-042's `--decision ../../etc/passwd` case lacked.
  Row 4 correctly adds the `petitiones/A-1.md` byte-identity assertion, since
  answer's own discriminator is A-1 and not the sentinels.

## 8. Reds — headers, baselines, protocol

  01.log  # behaviour: 1  # exit: 1  # sella: builder-b  # command: node scripts/safe-item-path-mutants.mjs contract
          baseline: 0 FAIL — --import tsx packages/cli/src/writes.test.ts .
          4 banners, kills labels on ^FAIL lines, mD alive. 70 lines.
  02.log  # behaviour: 2  # exit: 1  # sella: builder-b  # command: ... writes
          baseline line present before the first mutant. 10 banners. 92 lines.
  03.log  # behaviour: 3  # exit: 1  # sella: builder-b  # command: ... lifecycle
          baseline names BISELLIUM_ONLY_BEHAVIOUR=45. 8 banners. 66 lines.
  04.log  # behaviour: 4  # exit: 1  # sella: builder-b  # command: ... census
          3 banners; mG alive. 30 lines.
  Every `# command:` names a committed path (scripts/), never a scratchpad —
  W-042 round 2's advisory A-1 is satisfied.

  The harness's protocol matches the brief where it matters, and I read it rather
  than trusting the logs: baseline per distinct test command before any mutation
  with exit 3 on failure; `died = exitCode !== 0 && every kills label seen on a
  ^FAIL line`, so exit code alone never kills; restore from in-memory originals in
  a `finally` with a byte-equality re-read and exit 3 on mismatch, never
  `git checkout --`; empty group exit 2; inverted exits 1/0. My own `all` run
  left porcelain empty, which exercises the restore path 33 times.

  Red 04's content is where F-1 lands: its mF banner demonstrates the "Id" filter,
  not inventory B's specified property.

